\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {14.1.5}Worked Example: a $V=3$ Knapsack on $T$}{844}{subsection.14.1.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{ex:c27-knap-run}{{14.1.3}{844}{Top-$j$ label-sum knapsack on the running tree $T$}{tcb@cnt@definition.14.1.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1.1}{\ignorespaces The \emph  {merge cost ledger} for $T$ at $V=3$. Each non-root edge carries the capped count of folding its child into the parent: the product of $\min (S_{v,c},3)$ and $\min (\size  (c),3)$. Over the nine edges the total is $28 \le 30 = Vn$, exactly Theorem~\ref {thm:c27-amortize}'s promise. Drop the cap (loop heads at raw sizes) and the merge costs $45$ on $T$ -- and $\Theta (n^2)$ on a chain.}}{844}{figure.caption.143}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c27-mergetree}{{14.1.1}{844}{The \emph {merge cost ledger} for $T$ at $V=3$. Each non-root edge carries the capped count of folding its child into the parent: the product of $\min (S_{v,c},3)$ and $\min (\size (c),3)$. Over the nine edges the total is $28 \le 30 = Vn$, exactly \thmref {c27-amortize}'s promise. Drop the cap (loop heads at raw sizes) and the merge costs $45$ on $T$ -- and $\Theta (n^2)$ on a chain}{figure.caption.143}{}}
